WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:11.140
Structural validation tells the application whether data has the expected shape, but it does not prove who created the data or whether it was modified outside the application's trust model.

2
00:00:11.240 --> 00:00:17.960
Some serialized data needs integrity checks, authenticated messages, signatures, encryption, or secure transport.

3
00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:21.340
The right protection depends on what the data controls and where it travels.

4
00:00:21.620 --> 00:00:26.660
A plain checksum may detect accidental corruption, but it does not authenticate a sender.

5
00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:36.680
When origin and integrity matter, approved mechanisms such as HMACs, digital signatures, or authenticated secure channels may be appropriate under project policy.

6
00:00:36.780 --> 00:00:44.140
These checks should cover the exact data and context the application relies on, with clear rules for versioning and canonical representation.

7
00:00:44.420 --> 00:00:49.940
Encryption protects confidentiality, but encrypted data still needs validation after decryption.

8
00:00:50.040 --> 00:00:57.560
Decryption does not make a message well formed, current, authorized, or safe to construct into runtime objects.

9
00:00:57.660 --> 00:01:05.820
The application should still enforce schemas, limits, identity rules, compatibility requirements, and object invariants after the data becomes readable.

10
00:01:06.100 --> 00:01:10.260
Keys, certificates, and trust policy need lifecycle management.

11
00:01:10.360 --> 00:01:14.520
Use approved cryptographic libraries and avoid custom constructions.

12
00:01:14.620 --> 00:01:20.300
Review how keys are generated, stored, rotated, revoked, and logged.

13
00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:28.400
Serialization security is strongest when parsing controls and cryptographic controls are layered, tested, and owned together.

14
00:01:28.680 --> 00:01:31.480
Teams should document what each protection proves.

15
00:01:31.580 --> 00:01:37.420
Integrity, authenticity, confidentiality, and freshness are different properties.

16
00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:44.560
Naming the property helps reviewers see whether the selected control matches the risk instead of assuming one mechanism solves every problem.
